explain.narrative:
## Core concept
HAVING filters groups after GROUP BY has formed them. It is commonly used with aggregate expressions such as COUNT, SUM and AVG. WHERE filters individual input rows before grouping; HAVING filters the resulting groups.

## Syntax
```sql
SELECT grouping_column, aggregate_function(value_column)
FROM table_name
GROUP BY grouping_column
HAVING aggregate_condition;
```

## SQLite sample database
The following examples use a small fictional dataset. Each worked example starts with a repeatable setup script so it can be run independently in the SQL Playground.

## Worked examples
### 1. Show customers with at least two orders
```sql
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS orders;
CREATE TABLE orders (order_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, customer_name TEXT NOT NULL, city TEXT NOT NULL, amount NUMERIC NOT NULL);
INSERT INTO orders VALUES (1,'Asha','Rajahmundry',120),(2,'Bilal','Hyderabad',200),(3,'Asha','Rajahmundry',80),(4,'Chitra','Vijayawada',150),(5,'Bilal','Hyderabad',100),(6,'Dev','Mumbai',90);
SELECT customer_name, COUNT(*) AS order_count FROM orders GROUP BY customer_name HAVING COUNT(*) >= 2 ORDER BY customer_name;
```
Expected result: Asha | 2; Bilal | 2

HAVING evaluates the order count for each customer group.

### 2. Show cities whose sales exceed a threshold
```sql
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS orders;
CREATE TABLE orders (order_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, customer_name TEXT NOT NULL, city TEXT NOT NULL, amount NUMERIC NOT NULL);
INSERT INTO orders VALUES (1,'Asha','Rajahmundry',120),(2,'Bilal','Hyderabad',200),(3,'Asha','Rajahmundry',80),(4,'Chitra','Vijayawada',150),(5,'Bilal','Hyderabad',100),(6,'Dev','Mumbai',90);
SELECT city, SUM(amount) AS city_total FROM orders GROUP BY city HAVING SUM(amount) > 150 ORDER BY city;
```
Expected result: Hyderabad | 300; Rajahmundry | 200

Only city groups whose total is greater than 150 remain.

### 3. Combine WHERE and HAVING
```sql
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS orders;
CREATE TABLE orders (order_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, customer_name TEXT NOT NULL, city TEXT NOT NULL, amount NUMERIC NOT NULL);
INSERT INTO orders VALUES (1,'Asha','Rajahmundry',120),(2,'Bilal','Hyderabad',200),(3,'Asha','Rajahmundry',80),(4,'Chitra','Vijayawada',150),(5,'Bilal','Hyderabad',100),(6,'Dev','Mumbai',90);
SELECT city, SUM(amount) AS qualifying_total FROM orders WHERE amount >= 100 GROUP BY city HAVING SUM(amount) >= 150 ORDER BY city;
```
Expected result: Hyderabad | 300; Vijayawada | 150

WHERE removes small individual orders first; HAVING then filters the remaining city totals.

### 4. Filter groups using an average
```sql
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS orders;
CREATE TABLE orders (order_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, customer_name TEXT NOT NULL, city TEXT NOT NULL, amount NUMERIC NOT NULL);
INSERT INTO orders VALUES (1,'Asha','Rajahmundry',120),(2,'Bilal','Hyderabad',200),(3,'Asha','Rajahmundry',80),(4,'Chitra','Vijayawada',150),(5,'Bilal','Hyderabad',100),(6,'Dev','Mumbai',90);
SELECT city, ROUND(AVG(amount), 2) AS average_order FROM orders GROUP BY city HAVING AVG(amount) >= 125 ORDER BY city;
```
Expected result: Hyderabad | 150; Vijayawada | 150

HAVING can filter groups by an average aggregate.

### 5. Keep groups whose order count is not one
```sql
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS orders;
CREATE TABLE orders (order_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, customer_name TEXT NOT NULL, city TEXT NOT NULL, amount NUMERIC NOT NULL);
INSERT INTO orders VALUES (1,'Asha','Rajahmundry',120),(2,'Bilal','Hyderabad',200),(3,'Asha','Rajahmundry',80),(4,'Chitra','Vijayawada',150),(5,'Bilal','Hyderabad',100),(6,'Dev','Mumbai',90);
SELECT customer_name, COUNT(*) AS order_count FROM orders GROUP BY customer_name HAVING COUNT(*) <> 1 ORDER BY customer_name;
```
Expected result: Asha | 2; Bilal | 2

The condition removes one-order customer groups.

## Query behaviour and dialect notes
The examples target SQLite and use portable core SQL syntax. Column positions and compatible data types must align across set-operation branches. Result order is not guaranteed without an outer ORDER BY.

## Common mistakes
- Selecting a non-aggregated column that is not grouped.
- Confusing row-level WHERE filtering with group-level HAVING filtering.
- Forgetting that WHERE is applied before grouping.
- Grouping by too few columns for the intended report.
- Assuming groups are returned in a particular order without ORDER BY.

## Best practices
Use explicit aliases and readable predicates. Test edge cases such as missing matches, duplicate values, empty sets and NULLs. Keep the database dialect visible whenever a feature is not portable.

